<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-overall> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Cartesian transformation of the human skull into chimpanzee, baboon and dog (Figs. 404-408) — MorphoEval overall 72.6/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "v2 keeps the v1 reconstruction (bending 2.10, det A 0.54, log-area R^2 0.95 with beta_i +0.40 / beta_j -0.61) and adds the statistics the Pass 1 audit asked for. Headline: Thompson's drawn human->chimpanzee net removes 35 % of the Procrustes distance from the Fig. 404 human to a real chimpanzee skull; jitter bootstrap 0.30 [0.19, 0.41], P(E > 0) = 1.000; label-shuffled null mean 0.00 +/- 0.03, p = 0.0005; r 0.92 -> 0.97, RMSE 0.116 -> 0.075, 10/11 landmarks improved (Wilcoxon p 0.012, KS p 0.075). Controls: affine box stretch -0.12, mirrored net -0.60, specificity +0.11 (P 0.946 that the chimpanzee is the best-helped target). Along Thompson's own ray the real chimpanzee is best matched at k* = 0.85 (he drew 1.0), the baboon at 1.15 and the dog at 1.30 - \"increased intensity\" is literally recovered - but the baboon's displacement is only 76 % [69, 82] co-directional with the chimpanzee's, so \"precisely the same order\" is too strong (C2 mixed). Segmented silhouettes: the dog is the most triangular (taper 0.88 vs 0.94 / 0.97 / 1.06) and the silhouette face fraction orders H 0.29 < C 0.37 < B 0.53. Facial angles 89 / 68 / 56 / 45 deg with P 0.94 for the H > C > B ordering; all real-specimen orderings also hold with the Fig. 404 drawing as the human reference. Verdict 16/17 checks: supported (C1, C3, C4, C5, C6 supported; C2 mixed). Overall 72.6/100. Overall 72.6/100 (faithfulness 70.0, reproducibility 78.7); rubric morphoeval 0.1.0; checkpoint commit a5007b9c0; environment: . Dimension assessments: Model fidelity to source 74, Morphological realism / specimen match 66, Parameter expressivity 72, Morphometric validation 70, Source and data traceability 85, Code-to-model traceability 78, Reproducibility package 74. Output judged: docs/media/t1917_058_on_the_theory_of_transformatio_comparison.png: rendered output of thompson/claims/t1917-058_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py at a5007b9c0, image/png, 1145183 B, 1980×1650 px, artifact_sha256 93e8738afc53ea10e6742bc17e942de3a97ea0aa9b2ca037fe925ac628a97934 (sha256 93e8738afc53ea10e6742bc17e942de3a97ea0aa9b2ca037fe925ac628a97934), spec_fingerprint 974f3b1907dd69da0acb085b9f36dedce4c4532b025e0bf27519cd77a4567dc9. Dual provenance: spec_fingerprint: 974f3b1907dd69da0acb085b9f36dedce4c4532b025e0bf27519cd77a4567dc9; artifact_sha256: 93e8738afc53ea10e6742bc17e942de3a97ea0aa9b2ca037fe925ac628a97934; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/canis_familiaris_lateral.jpg=e5b1eab566faf5ed..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/fig404_human_skull_cartesian.jpg=568644185cca883a..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/fig405_chimpanzee_coordinates.jpg=7314ff685861068f..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/homo_sapiens_lateral.jpg=3457d096022c4b4f..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/pan_troglodytes_lateral.jpg=04455ef234b6ac4a..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/paper_citations.bib=f2b7ee8ec50937e4..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/papio_ursinus_lateral.jpg=02e80d49035d3940..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/scout_manifest.json=4aab29fefc62323b..., datasets/thompson_1917/t1917-058/t1917_058_on_the_theory_of_transformatio_reference.json=4fdcd4cba2786813...}. Supersedes audit: 2026-10-28-t1917-058-on-the-theory-of-transformatio-audit. Claim boundary: reported_in_source: Thompson (1917) pp. 770-773: the human skull drawn in a 5x4 Cartesian net (Fig. 404) and the chimpanzee, baboon and dog skulls drawn in deformed nets (Figs. 405-408); prose statements that the deformation is one 'harmonious and congruent transformation', that cell areas increase 'in approximately logarithmic order' from above downwards and behind forwards, that the baboon differs 'only in an increased intensity', that the chimpanzee is 'much nearer the human type than the baboon', and that the dog's net 'tapers away in front' on 'different lines'.; reconstructed_in_script: The Fig. 405 net digitised and reconstructed as a thin-plate spline T in human grid units (E1-E3); cell-area log-linear regression (E4); 11 homologous landmarks digitised by the loop agent on Fig. 404, Fig. 406 and four Commons specimens; full-Procrustes explained fraction of T(H) toward the real chimpanzee (E6) with jitter bootstrap, label-permutation null, r / RMSE / KS / Wilcoxon; lambda and k sweeps with k* scan; mirrored-net and wrong-target controls; silhouette segmentation taper index and face fraction; facial index and facial angle (E7).; transfer_boundary: One specimen per taxon (museum photographs for chimpanzee, baboon, dog; a rendered anatomical model for the human, whose influence is bounded by the Fig. 404-reference robustness check); the baboon and dog nets of Figs. 407-408 were not digitised, so C2/C5 are tested on real specimens (and, for C2, on the k* scan along the chimpanzee ray), not on Thompson's drawings of them.; does_not_claim: Nothing about growth, ontogeny or phylogeny; no claim that Thompson's curves are the optimal smooth map (a lambda-smoothed net does slightly better, E 0.38 vs 0.35); no claim that the baboon lies on exactly the chimpanzee ray - C2 is reported as mixed; the verdict is for these four specimens and would need a population sample to generalise.." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-rubric-0.1.0> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/claude-fable-5.1> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/overall" ;
    pk:evidenceAgainst "remediation needed: {'id': 'R6', 'item': 'Independent second assessor (different provider) - both rounds are builder-scored'}",
        "remediation needed: {'id': 'R7', 'item': 'Population sample: >= 5 specimens per taxon (e.g. MorphoSource / Phenome10K lateral renders) so the bootstrap covers biological variation, not only digitisation error'}",
        "remediation needed: {'id': 'R8', 'item': \"Digitise the baboon and dog nets of Figs. 407-408 (manual node placement) to test C2/C5 on Thompson's own drawn transformations, not only on real specimens\"}",
        "remediation needed: {'id': 'R9', 'item': 'Replace the rendered human model with a museum photograph of a real Homo sapiens skull (Commons API was unavailable for most of this run)'}" ;
    pk:hasScore 7.26e-01 ;
    pk:hasVerdict "mostly faithful" .

<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-parameter-expressivity> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Cartesian transformation of the human skull into chimpanzee, baboon and dog (Figs. 404-408) — Parameter expressivity: 72/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Both morphospace parameters are now swept and their effects shown: k drives the predicted facial angle monotonically 89 -> 68 -> 55 -> 47 -> 43 -> 39 deg for k = 0 .. 2.5 and the explained fraction through a clear maximum (k* 0.85 for the chimpanzee); lambda drives bending monotonically to the affine limit. The GIF animates k = 0 -> 1 -> 2. Deduction: no third parameter (e.g. anisotropic intensity for face vs vault) is exposed, and the k and lambda effects are reported on the point estimate only, not under the jitter bootstrap." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-parameter-expressivity> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/claude-fable-5.1> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/parameter-expressivity" ;
    pk:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-parameter-expressivity-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-parameter-expressivity-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-parameter-expressivity-3> ;
    pk:hasScore 7.2e-01 ;
    pk:hasVerdict "mostly faithful" .

<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-058-repro-package> a pk:LLMClaimAssessment ;
    rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Cartesian transformation of the human skull into chimpanzee, baboon and dog (Figs. 404-408) — Reproducibility package: 74/100" ;
    dct:date "2026-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:description "Deterministic (seed 1917 for bootstrap and permutation; self-test asserts bit-identical bootstrap means across two runs), self-test 2.7 s, full run 7.5 s, documented CLI (--self-test, --no-gif, --lam, --k), outputs at documented paths, metrics and result JSON regenerated each run. Deductions: dependencies not pinned per script; Lean needs an elan toolchain; checkpoint is a feature-branch commit." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/morphoeval-0.1.0-repro-package> ;
    pk:assessedBy <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/model/claude-fable-5.1> ;
    pk:assessmentType "morphoeval/0.1.0/repro-package" ;
